WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.099
Inventory at risk is a brilliant

00:00:02.099 --> 00:00:04.560
example of these diagnostic muddy waters.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.239
Because it sounds so simple.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.859
It does. But if you go

00:00:07.859 --> 00:00:08.940
down to the warehouse and ask

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.780
the manager what inventory at risk

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.580
means, they will likely give you

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:14.059
a purely physical definition.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.040
Meaning what? To them it means

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.640
the physical count of stocks sitting

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.100
on a pallet is currently below

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.920
a hard minimum threshold at this

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.259
exact second. Okay, but if you

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:24.860
take that exact same phrase to

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.700
a supply chain planner, the planner

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:27.439
is going to give you a

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:29.120
temporal predictive definition.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:30.939
Right, because their job is looking

00:00:30.939 --> 00:00:33.600
ahead. Exactly. To them, inventory

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.940
at risk means that based on

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.680
current sales velocity and historical

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.120
trends, a predictive projection shows

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.580
that the stock will fall below

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.960
a required safety buffer at some

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.560
point before the next delivery truck

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.960
physically arrives. Those are two radically

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.580
different mathematical equations.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.400
Completely different database queries

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.220
triggered by the exact same three

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:52.600
words.
